trs":{"latex":"S_5"}},{"type":"text","text":" of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"G_n"}},{"type":"text","text":":"}]}]},"e":"(i) At n = 4, A₄ = 16 − 8 = 8 and G₄ = 2³ = 8, so the shared point is (4, 8). (ii) S₅ = 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16 = 31.","m":2,"df":"medium","tp":"Comparing sequences","st":"intersection and geometric sum","cfg":{"question_number":14,"paper_question_key":14,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"intermediate","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(i)","marks":1,"focus":"Intersection point"},{"label":"(ii)","marks":1,"focus":"Geometric sum"}],"response_tools":["math","rich_text"],"tool_labels":["math","rich_text"],"source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":15,"t":"single_choice","p":"Evaluate the exact sum of the finite geometric series $\\sum_{k=1}^{5}81\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{k-1}$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Evaluate the exact sum of the finite geometric series "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\sum_{k=1}^{5}81\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^{k-1}"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$S_5=81\\left[\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)^5-1\\right]\\div\\left(\\frac{4}{3}-1\\right)=243\\left(\\frac{1024}{243}-1\\right)=1024-243=781$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Finite geometric series","st":"evaluate a sigma sum","cfg":{"question_number":15,"paper_question_key":15,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":16,"t":"single_choice","p":"Consecutive positive terms $m-1$, $2m+1$, $7m-1$ form a geometric sequence. Find $m$ and common ratio $r$.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consecutive positive terms "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"m-1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"2m+1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"7m-1"}},{"type":"text","text":" form a geometric sequence. Find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"m"}},{"type":"text","text":" and common ratio "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},"e":"$(2m+1)^2=(m-1)(7m-1)$ gives $3m(m-4)=0$; positive terms require m = 4, so the terms are 3, 9, 27 and r = 3.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Geometric sequences","st":"solving for an unknown and ratio","cfg":{"question_number":16,"paper_question_key":16,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":17,"t":"single_choice","p":"The coordinate graph shows the partial sums $S_n$ of an arithmetic series for $n=1$ to $5$. Find term $u_4$ and common difference $d$.\n\n[Figure: Partial sums Sₙ of an arithmetic series]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The coordinate graph shows the partial sums "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" of an arithmetic series for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n=1"}},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"5"}},{"type":"text","text":". Find term "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_4"}},{"type":"text","text":" and common difference "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"d"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"graphBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"version":1,"title":"Partial sums Sₙ of an arithmetic series","domain":{"xMin":0,"xMax":6,"yMin":0,"yMax":70,"gridStep":null,"showGrid":true,"showAxes":true,"showCoordinates":true,"showLegend":false,"showIntersections":false,"equalScale":false},"points":[{"id":"p0","x":1,"y":5,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p1","x":2,"y":14,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p2","x":3,"y":27,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p3","x":4,"y":44,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p4","x":5,"y":65,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true}],"functions":[],"segments":[],"annotations":[],"strokes":[],"regions":[]}}}]},"e":"$u_4=S_4-S_3=44-27=17$ and $u_3=S_3-S_2=27-14=13$, so $d=17-13=4$.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Partial sums","st":"recovering a term and difference","cfg":{"question_number":17,"paper_question_key":17,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":18,"t":"single_choice","p":"An Allada cooperative harvests 30 crates of pineapples in Week 1, increasing by 6 crates per week. How many weeks give 756 crates in total?","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An Allada cooperative harvests 30 crates of pineapples in Week 1, increasing by 6 crates per week. How many weeks give 756 crates in total?"}]}]},"e":"$S_n=\\frac{n}{2}[60+6(n-1)]=3n^2+27n=756$, so $n^2+9n-252=(n-12)(n+21)=0$ and n = 12.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic series","st":"number of terms for a target total","cfg":{"question_number":18,"paper_question_key":18,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":19,"t":"single_choice","p":"Over 5 years, Plan P pays 120 000 FCFA in Year 1 (+40 000 FCFA/yr); Plan Q pays 30 000 FCFA in Year 1, doubling each year. Compare 5-year totals ($S_5$).","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 5 years, Plan P pays 120 000 FCFA in Year 1 (+40 000 FCFA/yr); Plan Q pays 30 000 FCFA in Year 1, doubling each year. Compare 5-year totals ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_5"}},{"type":"text","text":")."}]}]},"e":"S₅,P = (5/2)[240 000 + 4(40 000)] = 1 000 000 FCFA; S₅,Q = 30 000(2⁵ − 1) = 930 000 FCFA. Plan Q is 70 000 FCFA cheaper.","m":2,"df":"hard","tp":"Comparing financial plans","st":"arithmetic and geometric series","cfg":{"question_number":19,"paper_question_key":19,"paper_section":"A","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":2,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":20,"t":"open_ended","p":"Explicit & recursive sequences\n(a) Sequence A has explicit rule $u_n=2n^2-5n+7$. Calculate $u_1$ and $u_4$, and find algebraically which term equals 49. [2]\n(b) Sequence B satisfies $v_1=12$ and $v_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}v_n+3$ $(n \\ge 1)$. Compute $v_2$, $v_3$ and $v_4$ as exact fractions, showing each step. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explicit & recursive sequences","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Sequence A has explicit rule "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n=2n^2-5n+7"}},{"type":"text","text":". Calculate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_4"}},{"type":"text","text":", and find algebraically which term equals 49. [2]\n(b) Sequence B satisfies "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_1=12"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_{n+1}=\\frac{1}{2}v_n+3"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(n \\ge 1)"}},{"type":"text","text":". Compute "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_2"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_3"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"v_4"}},{"type":"text","text":" as exact fractions, showing each step. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) u₁ = 4, u₄ = 19; 2n² − 5n + 7 = 49 gives (n − 6)(2n + 7) = 0, so n = 6. (b) v₂ = 9, v₃ = 15/2, v₄ = 27/4.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Explicit & recursive sequences","st":"evaluating and solving a quadratic sequence; exact fractions","cfg":{"question_number":1,"paper_question_key":20,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Terms and the equation"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Exact recursive fractions"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Explicit & recursive sequences","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":21,"t":"open_ended","p":"Intersecting linear sequences\nTwo arithmetic sequences $L_n$ and $M_n$ are plotted for $n=1$ to $6$ on the coordinate grid shown.\n\n[Figure: Sequences Lₙ = 33 − 4n and Mₙ = 3n + 5]\n(a) Derive simplified explicit formulas for $L_n$ and $M_n$, and interpret their intersection point $(4, 17)$. [2]\n(b) Find the term number $n$ and value of the first negative term of $L_n$. [1]\n(c) Calculate the sum $S_{15}$ of the first 15 terms of sequence $M_n$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Intersecting linear sequences","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two arithmetic sequences "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"L_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"M_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" are plotted for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n=1"}},{"type":"text","text":" to "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"6"}},{"type":"text","text":" on the coordinate grid shown."}]},{"type":"graphBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"version":1,"title":"Sequences Lₙ = 33 − 4n and Mₙ = 3n + 5","domain":{"xMin":0,"xMax":7,"yMin":0,"yMax":35,"gridStep":null,"showGrid":true,"showAxes":true,"showCoordinates":true,"showLegend":true,"showIntersections":false,"equalScale":false},"points":[{"id":"p0","x":1,"y":29,"label":"L","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p1","x":1,"y":8,"label":"M","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p2","x":2,"y":25,"label":"L","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p3","x":2,"y":11,"label":"M","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p4","x":3,"y":21,"label":"L","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p5","x":3,"y":14,"label":"M","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p6","x":4,"y":17,"label":"L","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p7","x":4,"y":17,"label":"M","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p8","x":5,"y":13,"label":"L","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p9","x":5,"y":20,"label":"M","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p10","x":6,"y":9,"label":"L","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p11","x":6,"y":23,"label":"M","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true}],"functions":[],"segments":[],"annotations":[],"strokes":[],"regions":[]}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Derive simplified explicit formulas for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"L_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"M_n"}},{"type":"text","text":", and interpret their intersection point "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(4, 17)"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Find the term number "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and value of the first negative term of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"L_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [1]\n(c) Calculate the sum "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_{15}"}},{"type":"text","text":" of the first 15 terms of sequence "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"M_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Lₙ = 33 − 4n and Mₙ = 3n + 5; at n = 4 both equal 17. (b) 33 − 4n < 0 gives n > 8.25, so the first negative term is L₉ = −3. (c) M₁ = 8, M₁₅ = 50, so S₁₅ = 15/2(58) = 435.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Intersecting linear sequences","st":"graphical analysis and series","cfg":{"question_number":2,"paper_question_key":21,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":5,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Rules and intersection"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"First negative term"},{"label":"(c)","marks":2,"focus":"Sum of 15 terms"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","graph"],"paper_title":"Intersecting linear sequences","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":22,"t":"open_ended","p":"Alternating geometric sequence\nA geometric sequence satisfies $u_2=-18$ and $u_5=486$.\n(a) Find the common ratio $r$, first term $u_1$, and explicit formula $u_n$. [2]\n(b) Calculate $u_7$ and justify algebraically whether −13 122 is a term of this sequence. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alternating geometric sequence","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A geometric sequence satisfies "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_2=-18"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_5=486"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find the common ratio "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r"}},{"type":"text","text":", first term "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1"}},{"type":"text","text":", and explicit formula "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Calculate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_7"}},{"type":"text","text":" and justify algebraically whether −13 122 is a term of this sequence. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) r³ = 486/(−18) = −27, so r = −3; u₁ = 6 and uₙ = 6(−3)ⁿ⁻¹. (b) u₇ = 6(−3)⁶ = 4 374. 6(−3)ⁿ⁻¹ = −13 122 gives (−3)ⁿ⁻¹ = (−3)⁷, so n = 8; yes, it is the 8th term.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Alternating geometric sequence","st":"ratio, rule and term test","cfg":{"question_number":3,"paper_question_key":22,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Ratio and formula"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Seventh term and term test"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Alternating geometric sequence","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":23,"t":"open_ended","p":"Quadratic sequence from differences\nFive terms of a quadratic sequence $u_n=an^2+bn+c$ are given below:\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) Construct a complete first- and second-difference table to find $a$, $b$ and $c$, and state $u_n$. [2]\n(b) Calculate $u_{10}$ and solve algebraically for $n$ when $u_n=151$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Quadratic sequence from differences","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Five terms of a quadratic sequence "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n=an^2+bn+c"}},{"type":"text","text":" are given below:"}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["Term n","1","2","3","4","5"],"rows":[["Value uₙ","−1","4","13","26","43"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Construct a complete first- and second-difference table to find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"a"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"b"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"c"}},{"type":"text","text":", and state "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Calculate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_{10}"}},{"type":"text","text":" and solve algebraically for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" when "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n=151"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) First differences 5, 9, 13, 17; second differences 4. 2a = 4 → a = 2; 3a + b = 5 → b = −1; a + b + c = −1 → c = −2, so uₙ = 2n² − n − 2. (b) u₁₀ = 188; 2n² − n − 2 = 151 gives (n − 9)(2n + 17) = 0, so n = 9.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Quadratic sequence from differences","st":"difference table and solving","cfg":{"question_number":4,"paper_question_key":23,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Differences and formula"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Tenth term and equation"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Quadratic sequence from differences","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":24,"t":"open_ended","p":"Evaluating finite series\nFor each finite series below, find the number of terms $n$ and evaluate the exact sum $S_n$:\n(a) Arithmetic series: 7 + 13 + 19 + … + 139. [3]\n(b) Alternating geometric series: 486 − 324 + 216 − … − 64. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Evaluating finite series","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For each finite series below, find the number of terms "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and evaluate the exact sum "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_n"}},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Arithmetic series: 7 + 13 + 19 + … + 139. [3]\n(b) Alternating geometric series: 486 − 324 + 216 − … − 64. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 7 + 6(n − 1) = 139 gives n = 23; S₂₃ = 23/2(7 + 139) = 1 679. (b) r = −2/3; 486(−2/3)ⁿ⁻¹ = −64 gives n = 6; S₆ = 486(1 − (−2/3)⁶)/(1 + 2/3) = 266.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Evaluating finite series","st":"number of terms and exact sums","cfg":{"question_number":5,"paper_question_key":24,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"A","criterion_label":"Criterion A — Knowing and understanding","criterion_title":"Knowing and understanding","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":5,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":3,"focus":"Arithmetic count and sum"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Geometric count and sum"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Evaluating finite series","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":25,"t":"open_ended","p":"Solar terrace pattern & quadratic graph\nStage $n$ has an $n \\times n$ green core ($C_n$) flanked by two border columns of $n$ pale panels each ($B_n$), giving total panels $T_n=C_n+B_n$.\n(a) Tabulate $B_n$, $C_n$ and $T_n$ for $1 \\le n \\le 5$, and classify $B_n$ and $T_n$ using differences. [2]\n(b) Write explicit rules for $B_n$ and $T_n$. On a coordinate grid in your booklet ($1 \\le n \\le 5$), plot both $(n,B_n)$ and $(n,C_n)$ and identify the stage where $C_n$ first exceeds $B_n$. [2]\n(c) Find which stage uses $T_n=168$ panels, and link the upward curvature of the $T_n$ graph to its first differences $T_{n+1}-T_n$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solar terrace pattern & quadratic graph","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stage "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" has an "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n \\times n"}},{"type":"text","text":" green core ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"C_n"}},{"type":"text","text":") flanked by two border columns of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":" pale panels each ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"B_n"}},{"type":"text","text":"), giving total panels "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_n=C_n+B_n"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Tabulate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"B_n"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"C_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"1 \\le n \\le 5"}},{"type":"text","text":", and classify "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"B_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" using differences. [2]\n(b) Write explicit rules for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"B_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". On a coordinate grid in your booklet ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"1 \\le n \\le 5"}},{"type":"text","text":"), plot both "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(n,B_n)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(n,C_n)"}},{"type":"text","text":" and identify the stage where "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"C_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" first exceeds "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"B_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(c) Find which stage uses "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_n=168"}},{"type":"text","text":" panels, and link the upward curvature of the "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" graph to its first differences "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"T_{n+1}-T_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) B: 2, 4, 6, 8, 10 (constant first difference); C: 1, 4, 9, 16, 25; T: 3, 8, 15, 24, 35 (constant second difference 2). (b) Bₙ = 2n, Tₙ = n² + 2n; Cₙ first exceeds Bₙ at Stage 3 (9 > 6). (c) n² + 2n = 168 gives Stage 12. Tₙ₊₁ − Tₙ = 2n + 3 increases by 2 each step, so consecutive secant slopes steepen and the graph curves upward.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"Pattern investigation","st":"solar terrace pattern and quadratic graph","cfg":{"question_number":6,"paper_question_key":25,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":6,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Table and classification"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Rules and graph"},{"label":"(c)","marks":2,"focus":"Stage and curvature"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","graph","table"],"paper_title":"Solar terrace pattern & quadratic graph","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":26,"t":"open_ended","p":"Affine recursive investigation\nInvestigate: $u_1=7$ → $u_{n+1}=2u_n-3$ (for $n \\ge 1$).\n(a) Compute $u_2$, $u_3$, $u_4$, $u_5$ and show that $\\Delta u_n=u_{n+1}-u_n$ forms a geometric sequence, stating its first term and ratio. [2]\n(b) Let $w_n=u_n-3$. Prove algebraically that $w_{n+1}=2w_n$, deduce the explicit formula for $u_n$, and verify it for $n=5$. [3]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Affine recursive investigation","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Investigate: "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1=7"}},{"type":"text","text":" → "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_{n+1}=2u_n-3"}},{"type":"text","text":" (for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n \\ge 1"}},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Compute "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_2"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_3"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_4"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_5"}},{"type":"text","text":" and show that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"\\Delta u_n=u_{n+1}-u_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" forms a geometric sequence, stating its first term and ratio. [2]\n(b) Let "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"w_n=u_n-3"}},{"type":"text","text":". Prove algebraically that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"w_{n+1}=2w_n"}},{"type":"text","text":", deduce the explicit formula for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_n"}},{"type":"text","text":", and verify it for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n=5"}},{"type":"text","text":". [3]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 11, 19, 35, 67; first differences 4, 8, 16, 32 have ratio 2. (b) wₙ₊₁ = uₙ₊₁ − 3 = 2uₙ − 6 = 2wₙ, with w₁ = 4, so wₙ = 4(2)ⁿ⁻¹ = 2ⁿ⁺¹ and uₙ = 2ⁿ⁺¹ + 3; at n = 5, 64 + 3 = 67.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Affine recursive investigation","st":"geometric first differences and closed form","cfg":{"question_number":7,"paper_question_key":26,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":5,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Terms and difference sequence"},{"label":"(b)","marks":3,"focus":"Proof, formula and check"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Affine recursive investigation","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":27,"t":"open_ended","p":"Arithmetic vs geometric growth\nArithmetic sequence $A_n$ and geometric sequence $G_n$ share $A_1=G_1=6$ and $A_2=G_2=9$.\n(a) Write explicit and recursive definitions (with initial terms) for both $A_n$ and $G_n$. [2]\n(b) Tabulate $n=1..5$, find the percentage by which $G_5$ exceeds $A_5$, and explain why $G_n>A_n$ for all $n \\ge 3$. [3]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Arithmetic vs geometric growth","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Arithmetic sequence "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"A_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and geometric sequence "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"G_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" share "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"A_1=G_1=6"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"A_2=G_2=9"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write explicit and recursive definitions (with initial terms) for both "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"A_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"G_n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Tabulate "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n=1..5"}},{"type":"text","text":", find the percentage by which "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"G_5"}},{"type":"text","text":" exceeds "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"A_5"}},{"type":"text","text":", and explain why "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"G_n>A_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" for all "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n \\ge 3"}},{"type":"text","text":". [3]"}]}]},"e":"(a) A: d = 3, Aₙ = 3n + 3, A₁ = 6, Aₙ₊₁ = Aₙ + 3. G: r = 1.5, Gₙ = 6(1.5)ⁿ⁻¹, G₁ = 6, Gₙ₊₁ = 1.5Gₙ. (b) A: 6, 9, 12, 15, 18; G: 6, 9, 13.5, 20.25, 30.375. G₅ exceeds A₅ by (30.375 − 18)/18 × 100% = 68.75%. From n = 2, A grows by 3 while G grows by 0.5Gₙ ≥ 4.5, and that increment increases, so G remains larger for n ≥ 3.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Arithmetic vs geometric growth","st":"definitions, table and comparison","cfg":{"question_number":8,"paper_question_key":27,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"B","criterion_label":"Criterion B — Investigating patterns","criterion_title":"Investigating patterns","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":5,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Both definitions"},{"label":"(b)","marks":3,"focus":"Table, percentage and reasoning"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Arithmetic vs geometric growth","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":28,"t":"open_ended","p":"Connecting graphical & algebraic forms\nThree sequences are plotted below for $n=1,2,3,4,5$:\n\n[Figure: Three sequences plotted for n = 1 to 5]\n(a) Classify Graph I (16, −8, 4, −2, 1), Graph II (5, 2, 1, 2, 5) and Graph III (3, 5.5, 8, 10.5, 13), citing a distinct visual feature of each graph. [2]\n(b) Using proper subscript notation, write both an explicit formula and a complete recursive definition for Graph I and Graph II. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Connecting graphical & algebraic forms","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three sequences are plotted below for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n=1,2,3,4,5"}},{"type":"text","text":":"}]},{"type":"graphBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"version":1,"title":"Three sequences plotted for n = 1 to 5","domain":{"xMin":0,"xMax":6,"yMin":-10,"yMax":18,"gridStep":null,"showGrid":true,"showAxes":true,"showCoordinates":true,"showLegend":true,"showIntersections":false,"equalScale":false},"points":[{"id":"p0","x":1,"y":16,"label":"I","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p1","x":2,"y":-8,"label":"I","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p2","x":3,"y":4,"label":"I","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p3","x":4,"y":-2,"label":"I","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p4","x":5,"y":1,"label":"I","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p5","x":1,"y":5,"label":"II","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p6","x":2,"y":2,"label":"II","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p7","x":3,"y":1,"label":"II","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p8","x":4,"y":2,"label":"II","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p9","x":5,"y":5,"label":"II","color":"#1d4ed8","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p10","x":1,"y":3,"label":"III","color":"#b45309","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p11","x":2,"y":5.5,"label":"III","color":"#b45309","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p12","x":3,"y":8,"label":"III","color":"#b45309","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p13","x":4,"y":10.5,"label":"III","color":"#b45309","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p14","x":5,"y":13,"label":"III","color":"#b45309","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true}],"functions":[],"segments":[],"annotations":[],"strokes":[],"regions":[]}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Classify Graph I (16, −8, 4, −2, 1), Graph II (5, 2, 1, 2, 5) and Graph III (3, 5.5, 8, 10.5, 13), citing a distinct visual feature of each graph. [2]\n(b) Using proper subscript notation, write both an explicit formula and a complete recursive definition for Graph I and Graph II. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) I is geometric (alternating signs, halving amplitude); II is quadratic (symmetric U-shape with vertex (3, 1)); III is arithmetic (collinear, gradient 2.5). (b) I: uₙ = 16(−1/2)ⁿ⁻¹, u₁ = 16, uₙ₊₁ = −uₙ/2. II: uₙ = (n − 3)² + 1 = n² − 6n + 10, u₁ = 5, uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 2n − 5.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Connecting graphical & algebraic forms","st":"classification, explicit and recursive rules","cfg":{"question_number":9,"paper_question_key":28,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Classification with visual features"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Rules for I and II"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","graph"],"paper_title":"Connecting graphical & algebraic forms","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":29,"t":"open_ended","p":"Sigma notation & critiquing a series solution\n(a) Express 14 + 10 + 6 + 2 + … + (−38) in sigma (∑) notation, showing how the upper limit is obtained, and evaluate the sum. [2]\n(b) To sum 7 terms of 4 + 12 + 36 + …, Amina writes: “$S_7=\\frac{4(1-3^6)}{1-3}=1456$.” Identify her error using precise mathematical language and write a clear, corrected calculation of $S_7$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sigma notation & critiquing a series solution","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Express 14 + 10 + 6 + 2 + … + (−38) in sigma (∑) notation, showing how the upper limit is obtained, and evaluate the sum. [2]\n(b) To sum 7 terms of 4 + 12 + 36 + …, Amina writes: “"},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_7=\\frac{4(1-3^6)}{1-3}=1456"}},{"type":"text","text":".” Identify her error using precise mathematical language and write a clear, corrected calculation of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_7"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) uₖ = 14 − 4(k − 1) = 18 − 4k; 18 − 4k = −38 gives k = 14, so the sum is ∑ₖ₌₁¹⁴(18 − 4k) = 14/2(14 − 38) = −168. (b) The exponent on r must be the number of terms, 7, not 6; she computed S₆. Correctly, S₇ = 4(3⁷ − 1)/(3 − 1) = 2(2186) = 4 372.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Sigma notation & critique","st":"upper limit and correcting a series error","cfg":{"question_number":10,"paper_question_key":29,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Sigma form and sum"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Error and correction"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Sigma notation & critiquing a series solution","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":30,"t":"open_ended","p":"First-difference linearity proof\nLet $Q_n=an^2+bn+c$ $(a \\ne 0)$.\n(a) Expand and simplify $D_n=Q_{n+1}-Q_n$ to prove that ${D_n}$ is arithmetic, stating $D_1$ and its common difference in terms of $a$ and $b$. [2]\n(b) Explain why plotting $(n,D_n)$ yields collinear points of gradient $2a$. Hence find $Q_n$ if the graph of $(n,D_n)$ passes through $(1,7)$ with gradient 6 and $Q_1=4$. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"First-difference linearity proof","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Let "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_n=an^2+bn+c"}},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(a \\ne 0)"}},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Expand and simplify "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"D_n=Q_{n+1}-Q_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" to prove that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"{D_n}"}},{"type":"text","text":" is arithmetic, stating "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"D_1"}},{"type":"text","text":" and its common difference in terms of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"a"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"b"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Explain why plotting "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(n,D_n)"}},{"type":"text","text":" yields collinear points of gradient "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"2a"}},{"type":"text","text":". Hence find "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" if the graph of "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(n,D_n)"}},{"type":"text","text":" passes through "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"(1,7)"}},{"type":"text","text":" with gradient 6 and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"Q_1=4"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) Dₙ = a(n + 1)² + b(n + 1) + c − (an² + bn + c) = 2an + (a + b). The difference Dₙ₊₁ − Dₙ = 2a is constant, so {Dₙ} is arithmetic with D₁ = 3a + b. (b) Dₙ = (2a)n + (a + b) is linear in n, so the points are collinear with gradient 2a. 2a = 6 → a = 3; 9 + b = 7 → b = −2; 3 − 2 + c = 4 → c = 3, so Qₙ = 3n² − 2n + 3.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"First-difference linearity proof","st":"proving arithmetic differences","cfg":{"question_number":11,"paper_question_key":30,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Expansion and proof"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Gradient and reconstruction"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"First-difference linearity proof","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":31,"t":"open_ended","p":"Multi-sequence classification & justification\nClassify each sequence as arithmetic, quadratic or geometric, justifying with differences or ratios, and state exact $u_5$ for each.\n\n[Figure: table]\n(a) Classification and justification. [2]\n(b) Exact fifth terms. [1]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Multi-sequence classification & justification","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Classify each sequence as arithmetic, quadratic or geometric, justifying with differences or ratios, and state exact "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_5"}},{"type":"text","text":" for each."}]},{"type":"assessDiagramBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"kind":"table","title":"","columns":["ID","First four terms (u₁, u₂, u₃, u₄)"],"rows":[["Seq X","25, 19, 13, 7"],["Seq Y","24, 18, 27/2, 81/8"],["Seq Z","1, 6, 15, 28"]],"version":1}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Classification and justification. [2]\n(b) Exact fifth terms. [1]"}]}]},"e":"X: constant difference −6 (arithmetic), u₅ = 1. Y: constant ratio 3/4 (geometric), u₅ = 243/32. Z: first differences 5, 9, 13 and constant second difference 4 (quadratic), u₅ = 28 + 17 = 45.","m":3,"df":"hard","tp":"Multi-sequence classification","st":"difference/ratio justification and fifth terms","cfg":{"question_number":12,"paper_question_key":31,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","criterion_label":"Criterion C — Communicating","criterion_title":"Communicating","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":3,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Three classifications and justifications"},{"label":"(b)","marks":1,"focus":"All three fifth terms"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","table"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","table"],"paper_title":"Multi-sequence classification & justification","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":32,"t":"open_ended","p":"Zou irrigation canal construction\nEngineers line 45 m of canal in Week 1, increasing weekly progress by 8 m each week.\n(a) Find the length lined in Week 12 alone ($u_{12}$) and cumulative length after 12 weeks ($S_{12}$). [2]\n(b) The full canal measures 1 680 m. Form and solve a quadratic equation to find how many weeks are required to finish the project. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Zou irrigation canal construction","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Engineers line 45 m of canal in Week 1, increasing weekly progress by 8 m each week."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Find the length lined in Week 12 alone ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_{12}"}},{"type":"text","text":") and cumulative length after 12 weeks ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_{12}"}},{"type":"text","text":"). [2]\n(b) The full canal measures 1 680 m. Form and solve a quadratic equation to find how many weeks are required to finish the project. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) u₁₂ = 45 + 11(8) = 133 m; S₁₂ = 12/2(45 + 133) = 1 068 m. (b) Sₙ = n/2[90 + 8(n − 1)] = 4n² + 41n = 1 680, so 4n² + 41n − 1 680 = 0 = (n − 16)(4n + 105); n = 16 weeks.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Irrigation canal construction","st":"arithmetic model and quadratic equation","cfg":{"question_number":13,"paper_question_key":32,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Week-12 and cumulative lengths"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Quadratic model and solution"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Zou irrigation canal construction","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":33,"t":"open_ended","p":"Parakou solar plant output\nThe coordinate graph shows annual generation $E_n$ (MWh) in Years 1–4 as panels degrade by 5% per year.\n\n[Figure: Annual generation of the Parakou solar plant]\n(a) Verify from the plotted data that $E_n$ is geometric with $r=0.95$, state an explicit rule for $E_n$, and compute $E_6$ and $E_8$ (to 1 d.p.) to extend the graph. [2]\n(b) Calculate cumulative 8-year output $S_8$ (to 1 d.p.) to test whether a 3 300 MWh supply target is met, and find the first year when $E_n$ drops below the 340 MWh threshold. [3]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Parakou solar plant output","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The coordinate graph shows annual generation "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" (MWh) in Years 1–4 as panels degrade by 5% per year."}]},{"type":"graphBlock","attrs":{"spec":{"version":1,"title":"Annual generation of the Parakou solar plant","domain":{"xMin":0,"xMax":5,"yMin":400,"yMax":520,"gridStep":null,"showGrid":true,"showAxes":true,"showCoordinates":true,"showLegend":false,"showIntersections":false,"equalScale":false},"points":[{"id":"p0","x":1,"y":500,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p1","x":2,"y":475,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p2","x":3,"y":451.25,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true},{"id":"p3","x":4,"y":428.6875,"label":"","color":"#0a5c36","showCoordinates":true,"open":false,"visible":true}],"functions":[],"segments":[],"annotations":[],"strokes":[],"regions":[]}}},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Verify from the plotted data that "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" is geometric with "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r=0.95"}},{"type":"text","text":", state an explicit rule for "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E_n"}},{"type":"text","text":", and compute "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E_6"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E_8"}},{"type":"text","text":" (to 1 d.p.) to extend the graph. [2]\n(b) Calculate cumulative 8-year output "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_8"}},{"type":"text","text":" (to 1 d.p.) to test whether a 3 300 MWh supply target is met, and find the first year when "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"E_n"}},{"type":"text","text":" drops below the 340 MWh threshold. [3]"}]}]},"e":"(a) 475/500 = 451.25/475 = 0.95, so Eₙ = 500(0.95)ⁿ⁻¹; E₆ ≈ 386.9 and E₈ ≈ 349.2 MWh. (b) S₈ = 500(1 − 0.95⁸)/0.05 ≈ 3 365.8 MWh > 3 300, so the target is met by about 65.8 MWh. E₈ ≈ 349.2 ≥ 340 but E₉ ≈ 331.7 < 340, so the first year below threshold is Year 9.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"Solar plant output","st":"geometric decay, cumulative output and threshold","cfg":{"question_number":14,"paper_question_key":33,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":5,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Rule and two future terms"},{"label":"(b)","marks":3,"focus":"Cumulative total and threshold"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math","graph"],"paper_title":"Parakou solar plant output","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":34,"t":"open_ended","p":"Research endowment annuity\nA foundation invests $4 000 at the start of each year for 10 years at 5% compound interest per annum.\n(a) Explain why the end-of-Year-10 balance equals $4000(1.05)+4000(1.05)^2+\\cdots+4000(1.05)^{10}$, stating $u_1$, $r$ and $n$. [2]\n(b) Calculate the end-of-Year-10 balance (to 2 d.p.) and the total compound interest earned. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Research endowment annuity","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A foundation invests $4 000 at the start of each year for 10 years at 5% compound interest per annum."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Explain why the end-of-Year-10 balance equals "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"4000(1.05)+4000(1.05)^2+\\cdots+4000(1.05)^{10}"}},{"type":"text","text":", stating "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"u_1"}},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r"}},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"n"}},{"type":"text","text":". [2]\n(b) Calculate the end-of-Year-10 balance (to 2 d.p.) and the total compound interest earned. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) The 10th deposit earns one year of interest and the 1st earns ten years; the balance is a geometric series with u₁ = 4 000(1.05) = 4 200, r = 1.05, n = 10. (b) S₁₀ = 4 200(1.05¹⁰ − 1)/0.05 = $52 827.15; principal is $40 000, so interest is $12 827.15.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Compound-interest annuity","st":"geometric series in finance","cfg":{"question_number":15,"paper_question_key":34,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Explanation and parameters"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Balance and interest"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Research endowment annuity","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}},{"n":35,"t":"open_ended","p":"Comparing fleet logistics contracts\nOver 8 months, Contract A pays 240 000 FCFA in Month 1 (+35 000 FCFA/month); Contract B pays 150 000 FCFA in Month 1 (+25% per month, $r=1.25$).\n(a) Calculate total 8-month revenue ($S_8$) under Contract A and Contract B (nearest FCFA). [2]\n(b) Compare both options for a 4-month trial ($S_4$) versus the full 8-month term, advising the firm with figures. [2]","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comparing fleet logistics contracts","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 8 months, Contract A pays 240 000 FCFA in Month 1 (+35 000 FCFA/month); Contract B pays 150 000 FCFA in Month 1 (+25% per month, "},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"r=1.25"}},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate total 8-month revenue ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_8"}},{"type":"text","text":") under Contract A and Contract B (nearest FCFA). [2]\n(b) Compare both options for a 4-month trial ("},{"type":"mathInline","attrs":{"latex":"S_4"}},{"type":"text","text":") versus the full 8-month term, advising the firm with figures. [2]"}]}]},"e":"(a) S₈,A = 8/2[2(240 000) + 7(35 000)] = 2 900 000 FCFA; S₈,B = 150 000(1.25⁸ − 1)/0.25 ≈ 2 976 279 FCFA. (b) S₄,A = 1 170 000 and S₄,B ≈ 864 844: choose A for a 4-month trial (+305 156 FCFA), but B for the full 8 months (+76 279 FCFA) once compounding overtakes linear growth.","m":4,"df":"hard","tp":"Fleet logistics contracts","st":"comparing arithmetic and geometric totals","cfg":{"question_number":16,"paper_question_key":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","criterion_label":"Criterion D — Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","criterion_title":"Applying mathematics in real-life contexts","difficulty_level":"advanced","marks":4,"calculator":true,"calculator_policy":"permitted","assessment_style":"myp_summative","marking_breakdown":[{"label":"(a)","marks":2,"focus":"Both eight-month totals"},{"label":"(b)","marks":2,"focus":"Four-month comparison and advice"}],"response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"tool_labels":["rich_text","math"],"paper_title":"Comparing fleet logistics contracts","source":"myp5-term1-sequences-series-version-b-2026"}}]'::jsonb) as x
